{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/abstract-rule-vs-demo-heterogeneity.md", "content": "# Distinguish visual heterogeneity from task-rule drift

A fresh stream used demos with very different dimensions, palettes, backgrounds, and motifs from the test. The demos' observed edits were themselves disparate localized edits: demo 1 changed a pair of 5s to 6s; demo 2 changed a single 8 to 9; demo 3 changed a 7-cell to 9; demo 4 changed a local 7 region to 8; demo 5 changed two 7s to 3; demo 6 recolored a compact 2x3 patch from 2 to 5. No shared selector or abstract transformation was derived, and repeated requests were made without analyzing a cumulative delta ledger. The session's final candidate merely recolored the test's 8 structures to 4 without support from the demos.

Reusable inference lesson: neither assume that different-looking demos are unrelated nor force a common rule based on colors/salience. Precisely record each pair's delta, object context, and unchanged candidates; then seek measurable invariant relations (shape, relative position, local neighborhood, object role) across examples. Test any proposed rule by exact full-output replay on every demo. If deltas remain unlinked, further demos require a specific competing-hypothesis prediction; serial requests do not resolve uncertainty by themselves. For the test, keep its actual colors and structures unchanged unless a validated rule predicts edits.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/abstract-rule-vs-demo-heterogeneity.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-task-delta-inference", "file": "references/session-dabb45d0-review.md", "content": "# Session review: task-dabb45d0

The stream included edge bars and isolated/motif colors, but the sole supplied demo that changed cells changed only two adjacent 5s to 6s (demo 1: rows 2–3, column 1 in zero-based coordinates); demos 2–4 changed only small portions: demo 2 changed three 7s in a 7-bar to 4s, demo 3 changed a 3x2 patch of 8s to 7s, and demo 4 recolored a compact 5-shape to 6. These are distinct local recolorings, not evidence for projecting broad edge rays. Repeated submissions that altered a guessed 3-color edge pattern failed, illustrating the need to fit a selector or relation to all demos before transfer.

Operational lesson: compute each demo's exact delta, including changed old/new colors and unchanged candidates; do not infer common mechanics just from edge bars or marker presence. If hypotheses remain unvalidated, a demo is useful only if its geometry discriminates them. Do not submit a rule unsupported by exact demo replay. This note does not establish a solved transformation for this task.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-task-delta-inference/references/session-dabb45d0-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-stream-solving", "file": "references/expanded-output-mapping.md", "content": "# Expanded grids built from transformed input blocks

In some ARC tasks the output dimensions are an integer multiple of the input dimensions, and the output is a blockwise arrangement of transformed copies. Do not assume ordinary same-size editing or simple repetition. Partition the demo output into blocks matching the input's dimensions, then compare each block cell-by-cell with the input under rotations and reflections. Establish both the transform of each block and its position in the arrangement. Replay the complete mapping exactly on the demonstration before transferring it to a test input.

A 2x2 arrangement may use all four quarter-turn orientations, one per quadrant. Test orientation and block ordering independently; visually plausible patterns are insufficient. Once verified, construct each transformed block with explicit array operations and check the final dimensions and rows before submitting.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/continual-arc-stream-solving/references/expanded-output-mapping.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-demo-discipline", "file": "references/grid-serialization-validation.md", "content": "# Grid serialization check

A visually copied or manually assembled output can be invalid even when the task rule is understood. Before submission, verify mechanically that the output has exactly the expected number of rows and that each row has exactly the expected number of cells. Check values are integers in the allowed range, then serialize the same validated grid. Do not infer successful dimensionality from the visual layout: inspect all row lengths, since one omitted background cell in a row is enough to make a nominal 19x17 output malformed.
